#!/bin/bash
# Reads, for each tool a piece of work needs outside the project, whether it
# stands on this machine, and prints one line per tool and one on the
# install record, since 27 October 2026, decision (3) of the roadmap entry
# of that date on this program: the planning of two closing runs of 26
# October 2026 said nothing of a tool that was missing, and one wrote that a
# tool was installed without having read the machine, new defect 4 of the
# roadmap entry of 27 October 2026 on the three closing runs. So a program
# reads the machine, and a run says of a tool only what this program
# printed. At the tip of the branch that brings it, plan-work calls it: once
# in a round of Stage 1 before anything is said of a tool, and again where a
# tool becomes known after the round. The call of build-work before the
# start of the build subagent, and its read at the next start for an open
# issue that blocks a task on a missing install, come with the part on the
# build, and the forms --build and --installed below carry them.
#
# Usage: devloop-tool-state --planning TOOL ...
#        devloop-tool-state --build TOOL ...
#        devloop-tool-state --installed COMMAND TOOL
#        devloop-tool-state --self-test
#   in the project's directory: CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR, and without it the
#   current directory; a browser is read against the project's test tool
#   there, and the record off the project's main branch as last fetched.
#
# A TOOL is one of two things, as build-work step 3 point 7 has them. A
# command that lands outside the repository, named as it is called - yq,
# mmdc - read on the search path with command -v: the search path answers
# whether a tool stands there for use. Or a browser for tests, named
# VENDOR:NAME - playwright:webkit, playwright:chromium, playwright:firefox,
# puppeteer:chrome, puppeteer:firefox, puppeteer:chrome-headless-shell,
# cypress:binary, the one thing Cypress downloads - read in the folder the
# vendor names for this system under "Whether it worked is read off the
# result" in shared/backed-command.md, or in the folder a setting of the
# vendor names where one is set. A browser counts as found only in the
# version the project's test tool needs, and only a package in the
# project's own node_modules says which, since 28 October 2026: for
# Playwright its dry run names the version and the folder of that version,
# node_modules/.bin/playwright install --dry-run NAME, the project's own
# command run in the project - on Windows under Git Bash the shell script
# npm, pnpm and yarn write beside the .cmd - which honours
# PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH itself; for Cypress the version is the one of
# node_modules/cypress in the project and the folder <cache>/<version>,
# the cache CYPRESS_CACHE_FOLDER where it is set; for Puppeteer, where
# node_modules/puppeteer-core or node_modules/puppeteer stands in the
# project, node, run in the project and told to resolve from it, asks
# puppeteer-core for the build of the browser and @puppeteer/browsers for
# its folder under the cache, PUPPETEER_CACHE_DIR where it is set, and
# refuses a module whose real path lies outside the project's
# node_modules. Measured on 28 October 2026 on Linux, in a directory
# without node_modules and with a global Playwright on the search path,
# npx --no-install playwright install --dry-run webkit answered for the
# project with the global package's version, and node resolves a module
# from the directories above the project, from NODE_PATH and from its
# global folders as well; so a package elsewhere on this machine answers
# for nothing here. A Playwright installed with pip into a virtual
# environment, or a package in a sub-directory of the project, is not
# read, the line naming the path looked at. The vendors' configuration
# files are not read. Where the project has no package of the vendor to
# say which version it needs, and on a system for which the shared text
# names no folder - Windows among them - with no setting of the vendor
# set, the line says neither found nor not found but not read, with the
# cause: a run says of the browser that it could not be read and why, in
# the person's language and not in the program's words - the wording
# approved on 28 October 2026 stands in the roadmap entry of 27 October
# 2026 on this program - nothing is handed over and no issue is raised on
# a thing nobody read, "In doubt, a run with nobody there is not blocked"
# in docs/skill-conventions.md, and under a yes the vendor's own install
# leaves a browser that stands already as it stands.
#
# One line per TOOL, in the order given, each ending with the day it was
# read, so that the line can stand in a spec as it is:
#   TOOL: found at PATH (on YYYY-MM-DD)
#   TOOL: not found on the search path (on YYYY-MM-DD)
#   VENDOR:NAME: found at FOLDER, VERSION as the project's Vendor needs it (on YYYY-MM-DD)
#   VENDOR:NAME: not found: nothing stands at FOLDER, the folder of VERSION as the project's Vendor needs it (on YYYY-MM-DD)
#   VENDOR:NAME: not read, because CAUSE (on YYYY-MM-DD)
# A path under the home directory is written with ~, as
# bin/devloop-install-note writes a folder: the planning writes the line
# into the spec, and the spec stands on GitHub.
# Then one line on the install record, "record: ...". Under --planning the
# record is not read: the line says that whether the run installs a tool
# that is missing at the build or the person gets the command for it is
# decided by the record as it stands at the build, build-work step 3 point
# 7, and that the planning settles nothing of it - the answer of today is
# not printed, since the record can change before the build and the
# planning may not write who installs, plan-work Stage 1. Under --build the
# record is read through bin/devloop-install-record off the main branch as
# last fetched, as the install guard reads it, so that no second copy of
# the resolution of the default branch comes into being:
#   record: tools yes on origin/DEFAULT, answered DATE: a tool that is missing is installed by this run at the build, backed, with 600000 milliseconds on the call, build-work step 3 point 7
#   record: tools no on origin/DEFAULT, answered DATE: a tool that is missing is handed to the person as a backed command with both ways before anything is built against a stand-in, build-work step 3 point 7
#   record: none, because CAUSE: as under no, a tool that is missing is handed to the person as a backed command with both ways before anything is built against a stand-in, build-work step 3 point 7
#
# Under --installed, whether COMMAND, an install handed to the person, has
# installed TOOL: not on the search path, where an older copy from
# elsewhere looks like a success, but in the folder the installer writes
# to, read off the installer itself, "Whether it worked is read off the
# result" in shared/backed-command.md - $(brew --prefix)/bin for brew;
# $(npm prefix -g)/bin for npm with -g or --global, the prefix itself on
# Windows, where npm puts its commands; $(go env GOBIN), or $(go env
# GOPATH)/bin where GOBIN is empty, for go; pnpm bin -g; yarn global bin;
# bun pm bin -g; pipx environment --value PIPX_BIN_DIR; uv tool dir --bin;
# the EXECUTABLE DIRECTORY of gem environment; and for cargo, which has no
# query, $CARGO_INSTALL_ROOT/bin, $CARGO_HOME/bin or ~/.cargo/bin as cargo
# documents them. The installer is the first word of COMMAND after any
# VAR=value; a browser TOOL is read as above, the folder being the
# vendor's, whatever COMMAND says. The tool stands where TOOL, or on
# Windows TOOL.exe, TOOL.cmd or TOOL.bat, exists in the folder. The folder
# line first, and no record line:
#   TOOL: found at FOLDER/TOOL, the folder INSTALLER writes to, read off QUERY (on YYYY-MM-DD)
#   TOOL: not found at FOLDER/TOOL, the folder INSTALLER writes to, read off QUERY; a copy elsewhere on the search path does not count (on YYYY-MM-DD)
#   TOOL: not read, because CAUSE (on YYYY-MM-DD)
# then, for a command and since 28 October 2026, a second line, the
# search-path line as --build prints it, found at PATH or not found on the
# search path: the build and the checks call a tool through the search path,
# and go install puts a tool into ~/go/bin, which often stands on no search
# path, so that a command can have installed and the build still not find
# the tool; the run says so where the first line says found and the second
# not found, build-work step 3 point 7, the roadmap entry of 28 October 2026
# on the hook on SubagentStart. For a browser the one line stays: the
# vendor's folder is where the test tool looks.
#
# Every query of a vendor or an installer runs under bin/devloop-bounded
# with sixty seconds, so that the program ends inside the 120 seconds of a
# Bash call; a query that does not answer leaves its line not read, with
# the query and its message, never found; one not on the path answers "is
# not on PATH". The program fetches nothing and asks GitHub nothing; the
# default branch it does not resolve itself.
# Exit 0: every TOOL found, under --installed in the folder and on the
# search path both. 1: one or more not found or not read; the record line
# never changes the exit. 2 with one line on stderr: no form
# first or one it does not know, no TOOL, a TOOL that is neither a bare
# word nor VENDOR:NAME of one of the three vendors, --installed without
# exactly a COMMAND and a TOOL, an empty COMMAND, or a project directory
# that cannot be entered; --self-test alone is the one exception.
# --self-test produces every form of the lines and every exit code once,
# independent of the search path and of the folders of the machine it runs
# on: stand-ins for brew, npm, pnpm, yarn, bun, go, pipx, uv, gem, node
# and, as a global package would put them there, npx, playwright and
# cypress, which the self-test alone puts first on the path, answer from
# the case's script and log every call, and the project's own
# node_modules/.bin/playwright is the same stand-in where a case gives the
# project a Playwright; a home directory of its own holds the folders;
# DEVLOOP_TOOL_STATE_SYSTEM stands in for uname -s in the self-test alone;
# and a throwaway repository whose main branch as last fetched carries
# each case's record, committed under a fixed invented identity, stands in
# for the project. It prints one line per case, names
# every case that does not come out as this head says with what the
# program printed, and exits 1 at its end where any was red. It needs bash
# and no GNU tool; .github/workflows/stock-take.yml runs it with every
# self-test under bin/ on four systems.
set -u
me=devloop-tool-state

usage() { echo "$me: $1" >&2; exit 2; }

selftest() {
  local self T P out rc n=0 f=0 RED=0 LASTRED= DAY SYS=Darwin WITH= s
  self=$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)/$(basename "$0")
  T=$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/devloop-tool-state-selftest.XXXXXX") || exit 1
  trap 'rm -rf "$T"' EXIT
  # in the shell's own form, so that it can stand on the path: a drive letter's colon would split it
  T=$(cd "$T" && pwd)
  P=$T/project
  DAY=$(date +%Y-%m-%d)
  mkdir -p "$T/bin" "$T/home/bin" "$T/w" "$P/docs/agents" "$T/s"
  # The stand-ins. Each answers call N of its label from the case's script,
  # $T/s/LABEL.N, or from $T/s/LABEL.d where N has none; it logs every call
  # in order under its label and marks a call no answer was scripted for,
  # which makes the case red; as bin/devloop-start-conditions' stand-in for
  # gh does. On the path stand the installers, node, and - as a global
  # package would put them there, since 28 October 2026 - npx, playwright
  # and cypress, which no case may see asked; the project's own
  # node_modules/.bin/playwright is the same stand-in under the label
  # project-playwright, put there by pw below.
  cat > "$T/standin.sh" <<'IN'
#!/bin/sh
S=__S__
n=__N__
i=$(( $(cat "$S/$n.n" 2>/dev/null || echo 0) + 1 ))
echo "$i" > "$S/$n.n"
echo "$n $*" >> "$S/log"
a=$S/$n.$i
[ -e "$a.rc" ] || a=$S/$n.d
[ -e "$a.rc" ] || { echo "stand-in for $n: no answer scripted for call $i: $n $*" >&2; : > "$S/unscripted"; exit 1; }
cat "$a.out"; cat "$a.err" >&2
exit "$(cat "$a.rc")"
IN
  # standin PATH LABEL: the stand-in at PATH, answering and logging under LABEL
  standin() { sed "s|__S__|$T/s|; s|__N__|$2|" "$T/standin.sh" > "$1"; chmod +x "$1"; }
  for s in brew npm pnpm yarn bun go pipx uv gem npx node playwright cypress; do standin "$T/bin/$s" "$s"; done
  # the tool that stands on the search path of every case: an executable file under the self-test's home
  : > "$T/home/bin/devloop-selftest-present"; chmod +x "$T/home/bin/devloop-selftest-present"
  [ "$(PATH="$T/bin:$PATH" command -v npx)" = "$T/bin/npx" ] || { echo "FAIL the stand-in for npx is not the npx the path finds"; exit 1; }

  git_q() { git -c user.name=devloop-selftest -c user.email=selftest@example.invalid -c commit.gpgsign=false "$@"; }
  git_q init -q "$P" && git -C "$P" symbolic-ref HEAD refs/heads/main
  # record ENV: a commit of the throwaway project carrying docs/agents/environment.md holding ENV - no file where ENV is - - and origin/main and origin/HEAD set to that commit, as a fetch would leave them
  record() {
    rm -f "$P/docs/agents/environment.md"
    [ "$1" = - ] || printf '%s\n' "$1" > "$P/docs/agents/environment.md"
    echo "$n" > "$P/count"
    git_q -C "$P" add -A && git_q -C "$P" commit -q -m "commit $n" || { echo "FAIL the throwaway project could not be committed to"; exit 1; }
    git -C "$P" update-ref refs/remotes/origin/main HEAD
    git -C "$P" symbolic-ref refs/remotes/origin/HEAD refs/remotes/origin/main
  }
  YES='## Install permission
install-tools: yes
install-place: /opt/homebrew/bin
install-answered: 2026-10-25'
  NO='## Install permission
install-tools: no
install-answered: 2026-10-26'
  # fresh: a new script, every stand-in without an answer, the log empty, the folders of the home directory as they were made
  fresh() { rm -rf "$T/s" "$T/home/Library" "$T/home/.cache" "$T/home/cyp" "$T/home/pup" "$T/home/hb" "$T/home/npm" "$T/home/go" "$T/home/cg" "$T/home/.cargo" "$T/home/x" "$P/node_modules" "$T/node_modules"; mkdir "$T/s"; : > "$T/s/log"; SYS=Darwin; WITH=; }
  # pw: the project gets a Playwright, the stand-in standing as node_modules/.bin/playwright and answering under the label project-playwright
  pw() { mkdir -p "$P/node_modules/.bin"; standin "$P/node_modules/.bin/playwright" project-playwright; }
  # pkg NAME [VERSION]: the project gets the package NAME in its node_modules, with VERSION in its package.json
  pkg() { mkdir -p "$P/node_modules/$1"; printf '{\n  "name": "%s",\n  "version": "%s"\n}\n' "$1" "${2:-0.0.0}" > "$P/node_modules/$1/package.json"; }
  # outside NAME: the package NAME in the directory above the project, where node would resolve it from and the program may not
  outside() { mkdir -p "$T/node_modules/$1"; printf '{\n  "name": "%s",\n  "version": "1.0.0"\n}\n' "$1" > "$T/node_modules/$1/package.json"; }
  # ans NAME N RC OUT [ERR]: the answer to call N of NAME, d for every call N has none
  ans() { printf '%s\n' "$3" > "$T/s/$1.$2.rc"; : > "$T/s/$1.$2.out"; : > "$T/s/$1.$2.err"; [ -z "$4" ] || printf '%s\n' "$4" > "$T/s/$1.$2.out"; [ -z "${5:-}" ] || printf '%s\n' "$5" > "$T/s/$1.$2.err"; }
  # folder PATH [FILE]: the folder made, with FILE in it where one is named
  folder() { mkdir -p "$1"; [ -z "${2:-}" ] || : > "$1/$2"; }
  # run [ARGUMENT...]: the program, once, in the throwaway project, against the stand-ins, with the home directory of the self-test, SYS for uname -s and the variables of WITH
  run() { rundir "$P" "$@"; }
  rundir() { local d=$1; shift; ( cd "$d" && unset CYPRESS_CACHE_FOLDER PUPPETEER_CACHE_DIR CARGO_HOME CARGO_INSTALL_ROOT PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH NODE_PATH && PATH="$T/bin:$T/home/bin:$PATH" HOME="$T/home" CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR="$d" DEVLOOP_TOOL_STATE_SYSTEM="$SYS" GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES="$T" env ${WITH:+$WITH} "$self" "$@" ) > "$T/stdout" 2> "$T/stderr"; rc=$?; out=$(cat "$T/stdout" "$T/stderr"); }
  # fail NAME WHAT: the case NAME is red for WHAT; the program's output is printed once per red case, and every red case is named
  fail() { echo "FAIL $1: $2"; [ "$LASTRED" = "$1" ] || { printf '%s\n' "$out" | sed 's/^/    /'; LASTRED=$1; f=$((f + 1)); }; RED=1; }
  # calls NAME LINE...: the stand-ins were called with these lines, in this order, and with nothing else
  calls() { local name=$1 got; shift; got=$(cat "$T/s/log"); [ "$got" = "$(printf '%s\n' "$@")" ] || fail "$name" "calls '$(printf '%s' "$got" | tr '\n' ';')', expected '$(printf '%s;' "$@")'"; }
  # expect NAME CODE LINE...: the last run ended with CODE, printed every LINE whole and made no call its script did not answer
  expect() {
    local name=$1 want=$2 s; shift 2; n=$((n + 1))
    [ "$rc" -eq "$want" ] || fail "$name" "ended with $rc, expected $want"
    for s in "$@"; do printf '%s\n' "$out" | grep -qFx -- "$s" || fail "$name" "no line '$s'"; done
    [ ! -e "$T/s/unscripted" ] || fail "$name" "a call no answer was scripted for"
    [ "$LASTRED" = "$name" ] || echo "ok   $name"
  }
  # lines NAME COUNT: the last run printed COUNT lines on stdout
  lines() { [ "$(grep -c . < "$T/stdout")" -eq "$2" ] || fail "$1" "$(grep -c . < "$T/stdout") lines on stdout, expected $2"; }
  PRESENT="devloop-selftest-present: found at ~/bin/devloop-selftest-present (on $DAY)"
  ABSENT="devloop-selftest-absent: not found on the search path (on $DAY)"
  PLAN="record: not read here; whether the run installs a tool that is missing at the build or the person gets the command for it is decided by the install record as it stands at the build, build-work step 3 point 7, and the planning settles nothing of it"
  WAY="a tool that is missing is handed to the person as a backed command with both ways before anything is built against a stand-in, build-work step 3 point 7"
  DRY="WebKit 27.2 (playwright webkit v9999)
  Install location:    $T/home/Library/Caches/ms-playwright/webkit-9999
  Download url:        https://example.invalid/webkit-9999.zip

FFmpeg (playwright ffmpeg v1013)
  Install location:    $T/home/Library/Caches/ms-playwright/ffmpeg-1013"
  EXE="$T/home/.cache/puppeteer/chrome/mac_arm-156.0.1/chrome-mac-arm64/Google Chrome for Testing.app/Contents/MacOS/Google Chrome for Testing"

  # the arguments: exit 2 with one line on stderr
  fresh; run
  lines "no form first: 2" 0
  expect "no form first: 2" 2 "devloop-tool-state: expected --planning, --build or --installed first, got nothing"
  fresh; run --plan x
  expect "a form it does not know: 2" 2 "devloop-tool-state: expected --planning, --build or --installed first, got '--plan'"
  fresh; run --self-test --planning x
  expect "--self-test beside a form: not the self-test, 2" 2 "devloop-tool-state: expected --planning, --build or --installed first, got '--self-test'"
  fresh; run --planning
  expect "--planning without a tool: 2" 2 "devloop-tool-state: expected at least one TOOL after --planning"
  fresh; run --installed 'brew install yq'
  expect "--installed with one argument: 2" 2 "devloop-tool-state: expected exactly a COMMAND and a TOOL after --installed, got 1 arguments"
  fresh; run --installed '' yq
  expect "--installed with an empty command: 2" 2 "devloop-tool-state: the COMMAND of --installed is empty"
  fresh; run --planning devloop-selftest-present 'a b'
  lines "a tool that is no bare word: 2, nothing read before" 0
  expect "a tool that is no bare word: 2, nothing read before" 2 "devloop-tool-state: a TOOL is a bare word as it is called - letters, digits, dot, hyphen, plus and underscore - or VENDOR:NAME for a browser, got 'a b'"
  fresh; run --build -x
  expect "a tool starting with a hyphen: 2" 2 "devloop-tool-state: a TOOL is a bare word as it is called - letters, digits, dot, hyphen, plus and underscore - or VENDOR:NAME for a browser, got '-x'"
  fresh; run --planning chrome:webkit
  expect "a browser of a vendor it does not know: 2" 2 "devloop-tool-state: a browser is named VENDOR:NAME with playwright, puppeteer or cypress as the vendor, and cypress:binary for Cypress, got 'chrome:webkit'"
  fresh; run --planning cypress:app
  expect "cypress with a name other than binary: 2" 2 "devloop-tool-state: a browser is named VENDOR:NAME with playwright, puppeteer or cypress as the vendor, and cypress:binary for Cypress, got 'cypress:app'"
  fresh; run --planning 'playwright:'
  expect "a browser without a name: 2" 2 "devloop-tool-state: a browser is named VENDOR:NAME with playwright, puppeteer or cypress as the vendor, and cypress:binary for Cypress, got 'playwright:'"
  fresh; ( cd "$T" && CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR="$T/nowhere" HOME="$T/home" "$self" --planning devloop-selftest-present ) > "$T/stdout" 2> "$T/stderr"; rc=$?; out=$(cat "$T/stdout" "$T/stderr")
  expect "a project directory that cannot be entered: 2" 2 "devloop-tool-state: the project directory $T/nowhere cannot be entered"

  # --planning: the search path, the record not read, no git needed
  fresh; rundir "$T/w" --planning devloop-selftest-present devloop-selftest-absent
  lines "--planning: one found, one not, the record not read, outside any repository: 1" 3
  calls "--planning: one found, one not, the record not read, outside any repository: 1"
  expect "--planning: one found, one not, the record not read, outside any repository: 1" 1 "$PRESENT" "$ABSENT" "$PLAN"
  fresh; rundir "$T/w" --planning devloop-selftest-present
  lines "--planning: every tool found: 0" 2
  expect "--planning: every tool found: 0" 0 "$PRESENT" "$PLAN"

  # --build: the record through bin/devloop-install-record
  fresh; record "$YES"; run --build devloop-selftest-present
  lines "--build under a yes: the consequence of point 7, 0" 2
  expect "--build under a yes: the consequence of point 7, 0" 0 "$PRESENT" "record: tools yes on origin/main, answered 2026-10-25: a tool that is missing is installed by this run at the build, backed, with 600000 milliseconds on the call, build-work step 3 point 7"
  fresh; record "$NO"; run --build devloop-selftest-absent devloop-selftest-present
  lines "--build under a no: the handover with both ways, 1 on the tool" 3
  expect "--build under a no: the handover with both ways, 1 on the tool" 1 "$ABSENT" "$PRESENT" "record: tools no on origin/main, answered 2026-10-26: $WAY"
  fresh; record -; run --build devloop-selftest-present
  expect "--build with no record: the reader's cause, as under no, the exit by the tools alone" 0 "$PRESENT" "record: none, because there is no docs/agents/environment.md on origin/main as last fetched: as under no, $WAY"
  fresh; rundir "$T/w" --build devloop-selftest-present
  expect "--build outside any repository: the reader's cause" 0 "record: none, because this is not a git repository, so there is no default branch to read the record off: as under no, $WAY"
  record "$YES"

  # a browser of Playwright: the project's own command names the version and the folder; a package outside the project answers for nothing
  fresh; pw; ans project-playwright d 0 "$DRY"; folder "$T/home/Library/Caches/ms-playwright/webkit-9999" INSTALLATION_COMPLETE; run --planning playwright:webkit
  calls "playwright: the folder of the dry run stands, found" "project-playwright install --dry-run webkit"
  expect "playwright: the folder of the dry run stands, found" 0 "playwright:webkit: found at ~/Library/Caches/ms-playwright/webkit-9999, WebKit 27.2 (playwright webkit v9999) as the project's Playwright needs it (on $DAY)"
  fresh; pw; ans project-playwright d 0 "$DRY"; run --planning playwright:webkit
  expect "playwright: the folder absent, not found" 1 "playwright:webkit: not found: nothing stands at ~/Library/Caches/ms-playwright/webkit-9999, the folder of WebKit 27.2 (playwright webkit v9999) as the project's Playwright needs it (on $DAY)"
  fresh; pw; ans project-playwright d 0 "$DRY"; folder "$T/home/Library/Caches/ms-playwright/webkit-9999"; run --planning playwright:webkit
  expect "playwright: the folder empty, not found" 1 "playwright:webkit: not found: nothing stands at ~/Library/Caches/ms-playwright/webkit-9999, the folder of WebKit 27.2 (playwright webkit v9999) as the project's Playwright needs it (on $DAY)"
  fresh; ans npx d 0 "$DRY"; ans playwright d 0 "$DRY"; run --planning playwright:chromium
  calls "playwright: no package in the project, a global one on the path that would answer: not read, nothing asked"
  expect "playwright: no package in the project, a global one on the path that would answer: not read, nothing asked" 1 "playwright:chromium: not read, because the project has no Playwright package to say which version of chromium it needs, no node_modules/.bin/playwright (on $DAY)"
  fresh; pw; ans project-playwright d 1 '' 'Error: browserType.launch: Executable does not exist'; run --planning playwright:chromium
  expect "playwright: the project's command does not answer, not read" 1 "playwright:chromium: not read, because the project's Playwright did not say which version of chromium it needs: node_modules/.bin/playwright install --dry-run chromium did not answer: Error: browserType.launch: Executable does not exist (on $DAY)"
  fresh; pw; ans project-playwright d 127 '' 'env: node: No such file or directory'; run --planning playwright:webkit
  expect "playwright: the project's command finds no node, not read with its message" 1 "playwright:webkit: not read, because the project's Playwright did not say which version of webkit it needs: node_modules/.bin/playwright install --dry-run webkit did not answer: env: node: No such file or directory (on $DAY)"
  fresh; pw; ans project-playwright d 127 ''; run --planning playwright:webkit
